Brute Force – Sorting Every Time
**Complexity:**

Let n be the number of ropes.

- Sorting: O(n log n)
- Removing two elements: O(n)
- Inserting the new rope: O(1)
- Repeat at most n - 1 times.

**Overall Time Complexity:**

O(n² log n)

**Space Complexity:**

O(1) auxiliary space, ignoring sorting stack space.

class Solution {
public:
    long long minCost(vector<int>& arr) {

        long long cost = 0;

        while(arr.size() > 1) {

            sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());

            int a = arr[0];
            int b = arr[1];

            int sum = a + b;

            cost += sum;

            arr.erase(arr.begin());
            arr.erase(arr.begin());

            arr.push_back(sum);
        }

        return cost;
    }
};


//Better Approach – Find Two Minimum Elements
**Complexity:**

- Time Complexity: O(n²)
- Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(1).

**Why?**

- Finding the minimum element takes O(n).
- Erasing an element from a vector takes O(n) because remaining elements may need to be shifted.
- We repeat these operations up to n - 1 times.

Therefore, the overall time complexity is O(n²).

class Solution {
public:
    long long minCost(vector<int>& arr) {

        long long cost = 0;

        while(arr.size() > 1) {

            auto it1 = min_element(arr.begin(), arr.end());
            int a = *it1;
            arr.erase(it1);

            auto it2 = min_element(arr.begin(), arr.end());
            int b = *it2;
            arr.erase(it2);

            int sum = a + b;

            cost += sum;
            arr.push_back(sum);
        }

        return cost;
    }
};


